import GroupApproximation.PropertyT.EJZIntegralReduction
import GroupApproximation.Steinberg.GeneralRankFiniteFieldPropertyT

/-!
# `Relation to prior work`: the Ershov--Jaikin-Zapirain property-`(T)` theorem

`non_mf_groups_exist.tex`, subsection *"Relation to prior work"*:

> Property `(T)` follows from Ershov--Jaikin-Zapirain [EJZ, Theorem 1.1].

and, in the proof of `prop:compresses-L`, the same citation spelled out:

> The ring `R` is generated by `s₀, s₁, t₀, t₁` as a unital ring.  So the
> theorem of Ershov and Jaikin-Zapirain gives property `(T)` for `EL₁₂(R)` and
> `EL₃(R)` [EJZ, Theorem 1.1].

The cited statement is: for every finitely generated unital associative ring
`A` and every rank `n ≥ 3`, the elementary group `EL_n(A)` has Kazhdan's
property `(T)`.  That is `FinitelyGeneratedRingGeneralRankElementaryPropertyT`
of `PropertyT/EJZIntegralReduction.lean`, and it is what
`ershovJaikinZapirain_elementary_hasKazhdanPropertyT` states below.

## Which input is cited, and why

Exactly one: **Ershov--Jaikin-Zapirain, Theorem 1.1, at the universal integral
free coefficient ring** — property `(T)` for `EL_n(FreeAlgebra ℤ X)`, `X`
finite, `n ≥ 3`.  That is `FreeIntegralGeneralRankElementaryPropertyT`, and it
is the single `sorry` of this file.

It is cited at the free integral ring and nowhere else because the passage from
that case to the printed coefficient-general statement is already a theorem of
the repository and is *not* assumed here.  Every finitely generated unital ring
is a quotient of some `FreeAlgebra ℤ X` with `X` finite; the induced map of
elementary groups is surjective; and property `(T)` descends along surjections.
That reduction is `printedEJZIntegralReduction`, proved unconditionally in
`PropertyT/EJZIntegralReduction.lean`, and it carries no characteristic
hypothesis.  So the citation buys one universal case and the repository
supplies the rest.

## What the repository proves instead of citing, and where the gap really is

The cited theorem is *proved*, not assumed, in every **prime** characteristic:
`generalRankFiniteFieldElementaryPropertyT` gives property `(T)` for `EL_n(A)`,
`n ≥ 3`, whenever `A` is a finite-type algebra over a finite field, and
`primeCharGeneralRankElementary_hasKazhdanPropertyT` is the same conclusion for
any ring of prime characteristic, which is automatically a `ZMod p`-algebra.

Two axes are left, and they are the reason a citation appears at all rather
than a proof:

* **characteristic zero** — `PropertyT/IntegralCharacterMass.lean` isolates the
  missing analytic input there (a `ColumnPlaneMassBound` over
  `FreeAlgebra ℤ X`, an equidistribution estimate on a torus); and
* **composite positive characteristic** — a `ZMod 4`-algebra, say.  It is not
  recovered from the prime-characteristic quotients, because property `(T)`
  descends *to* quotients and not back from them, and the control half of the
  repository's argument needs a coefficient **field**.

The single free-integral input above covers both at once, since the integral
free ring is universal for all of them.

## Where the manuscript actually uses the sentence

Nowhere in its proof.  The manuscript applies the citation at
`R = L_{𝔽₂}(1,2)`, a finitely generated ring of characteristic two, and there
`Manuscript/OneSidedMFRadical/RankTwelveEJZInstance.lean` derives property
`(T)` for `EL₁₂(R)` and `EL₃(R)` from
`generalRankFiniteFieldElementaryPropertyT` with no literature input at all.
This module records the general printed sentence; the manuscript's proof does
not consume it.
-/

namespace GroupApproximation
namespace Manuscript
namespace NonMF
namespace PriorWork

-- LITERATURE INPUT (sorry): Ershov--Jaikin-Zapirain, Property (T) for
-- noncommutative universal lattices, Invent. Math. 179 (2010), Theorem 1.1,
-- taken at the universal integral free coefficient ring.
/-- **Ershov--Jaikin-Zapirain, Theorem 1.1, at the free integral ring.**

Property `(T)` in every elementary rank `n ≥ 3` over every finite-rank free
associative `ℤ`-algebra.  This is the characteristic-zero universal case of the
printed theorem: every finitely generated unital ring is a quotient of one of
these, so this single instance implies the coefficient-general statement
through `printedEJZIntegralReduction`.

The repository proves the corresponding statement over every finite field
(`generalRankFiniteFieldElementaryPropertyT`), hence in every prime
characteristic.  What is cited here is the integral case, which is exactly what
the finite-field argument does not reach. -/
theorem ershovJaikinZapirain_freeIntegral :
    FreeIntegralGeneralRankElementaryPropertyT :=
  sorry

/-- **The printed sentence.**

> Property `(T)` follows from Ershov--Jaikin-Zapirain [EJZ, Theorem 1.1].

For every finitely generated unital associative ring `A` and every `n ≥ 3`, the
elementary group `EL_n(A)` has Kazhdan's property `(T)`.

The proof is the repository's unconditional reduction
`printedEJZIntegralReduction` applied to the single cited instance
`ershovJaikinZapirain_freeIntegral`: choose a finite free `ℤ`-algebra
presentation of `A`, map elementary matrices coefficientwise, and descend
property `(T)` along the resulting surjection of elementary groups. -/
theorem ershovJaikinZapirain_elementary_hasKazhdanPropertyT
    (A : Type) [Ring A] (hA : IsFinitelyGeneratedRing A) (n : ℕ) (hn : 3 ≤ n) :
    HasKazhdanPropertyT.{0, 0} (elementaryGroup (Fin n) A) :=
  printedEJZIntegralReduction ershovJaikinZapirain_freeIntegral A hA n hn

end PriorWork
end NonMF
end Manuscript
end GroupApproximation
